\f0\fs56 \cf0 BRIIZOS.LOCAL AMBIENT REDESIGN PLAN\
\
\
ARCHITECTURAL OVERVIEW OF BRIIZOS.LOCAL\
\
Plain-language summary\
\
briizOS.local is already made of two broad parts.\
\
The visible part is the control center: the header, launcher, masonry tiles, drawers,\
floating tools, and full-screen views. It is drawn with React, the library used to build\
the visible page.\
\
The local engine is behind that interface. It stores information, runs the assistant,\
opens files, talks to macOS, captures screen memory, and returns results to the visible\
part. The masonry grid is not the engine. This distinction is what makes a relatively\
small first recast possible.\
\
A normal action follows this path:\
\
Person uses the visible interface\
    -> visible interface asks the protected bridge\
    -> protected bridge asks the desktop process\
    -> desktop process uses the local database, macOS, or the assistant\
    -> result returns through the bridge\
    -> visible interface shows the result\
\
The redesign should keep this working local path and replace the presentation controller\
that currently decides whether a feature is a tile, drawer, overlay, or floating surface.\
\
The answer to the first design question is that this can start small. The current visible\
application already has one place where tiled feature content is handed to the masonry\
container. Replacing that container can put several existing features into movable windows\
without changing their database or assistant behavior. It cannot finish the redesign by\
itself because other features live outside that container and several features own their\
own headers, dragging, resizing, full-screen behavior, and saved positions. The detailed\
source evidence appears later under WHY THIS CAN START SMALL BUT CANNOT END AS A WRAPPER.\
\
How to read this file\
\
The explanations are written to stand on their own. Every line beginning with Source is\
supporting code evidence and may be skipped unless a claim needs to be verified. The\
section named PROPOSED AMBIENT EXPERIENCE describes what the redesign will feel like. The\
numbered BUILD ORDER is the implementation appendix for the agent doing the construction.\
\
\
THE CURRENT VISIBLE WORKSPACE\
\
What a person experiences\
\
The main window opens as a control center. A launcher opens Chat, Sources, Tasks,\
Organizer, Calendar, Media, Notes, Canvases, Journals, Forest, Notefalls, briiZtorm,\
and settings. Some features enter the masonry grid. Others appear as drawers, floating\
surfaces, or views that cover most of the application.\
\
What makes it work\
\
App.jsx is the current presentation controller. It owns much more than the application\
start. It decides which surfaces are open, where each kind appears, how launcher actions\
find them, which surface Escape closes, and how canvas and journal presentations change.\
\
The masonry subsystem below App.jsx describes columns and vertical stacks. It moves,\
resizes, hides, restores, loads, and saves tiles. The stored dimensions are percentages\
within columns rather than free positions on the screen.\
\
