THE SHARED WINDOW FRAME\
\
Every ordinary feature window uses the same outer frame. The frame owns:\
\
- title and optional descriptive subtitle;\
- focus and front-to-back order;\
- moving and resizing;\
- minimize, close, Return Home, and Fill Screen;\
- minimum useful dimensions based on the feature's content;\
- screen-edge limits and recovery after display changes;\
- loading, empty, error, and confirmation placement;\
- saved local position and size.\
\
Return Home restores the feature's useful default size and centers it in the selected\
display's usable area. It controls window position only. It does not replace a feature's\
own Back, Back to Shelf, or Dock to Drawer action. A window can never become permanently\
unreachable beyond a screen edge.\
\
Fill Screen expands one feature window to the host's usable area and records its previous\
position and dimensions. Selecting Fill Screen again restores that previous placement.\
This preserves local's current full-screen panel behavior without entering macOS native\
full-screen mode.\
\
Close, minimize, Return Home, and Fill Screen remain perceivable without requiring hover.\
Tooltips use plain verbs. Decorative controls with no action are not included.\
\
Escape first closes the frontmost temporary menu or dialog. If none is open, it closes the\
frontmost window only when that window's current work can be dismissed safely. Unsaved or\
destructive work receives its existing confirmation.\
